W44-172 DC-tree e8 predictor-set perf A/B (Best vs Variable)
============================================================

Date: 2026-05-21
Status: SHIPPED
Production source change: jxl-encoder/src/vardct/{bitstream.rs, dc_tree_learn.rs}
  + new pub enum `PredictorSet { Variable, Best }` in dc_tree_learn.rs
    - `predictor_indices()` returns the slice of predictor IDs to evaluate
    - Variable: all 14 (libjxl `enc_ma.cc:543-547` swap order)
    - Best: only Weighted (6) + Gradient (5) (libjxl `enc_ma.cc:549`)
  + new pub fn `learn_dc_tree_best` and `learn_dc_tree_variable_with_set`
    - `learn_dc_tree_variable` now a 1-line forwarder to the `_with_set`
      core, so the existing Variable callers stay byte-identical
  + new const `DC_TREE_VARIABLE_PREDICTOR_FULL_MIN_EFFORT = 9` in bitstream.rs
    - dispatch at the W44-57 trial-and-pick site selects PredictorSet
      based on `effort >= DC_TREE_VARIABLE_PREDICTOR_FULL_MIN_EFFORT`:
        e8  → PredictorSet::Best (2 predictors)
        e9+ → PredictorSet::Variable (14 predictors)
  + bench-only env hook `JXL_W44_172_FORCE_VARIABLE_AT_E8` for A/B repro

Hardware: AMD Ryzen 9 7950X (32 cores), 128 GB RAM
Build: cargo build --release --features "__expert butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop parallel"
Decoder: jxl-oxide srgb_linear, fast-ssim2 (sRGB u8), butteraugli_linear (default)
Threads: 1 (single-threaded encode for fair A vs B compare; matches W44-170/W44-171 protocol)
Time iters per cell: 3, best-of-N reported

Cells (12):
  PROTECT_PERF (4) — target wedges:
    terminal e8 d=0.5  (1.7 MP screenshot)
    terminal e8 d=0.25 (1.7 MP screenshot)
    codec_wiki e8 d=0.5 (4.3 MP screenshot)
    clic_097cb426 e8 d=0.5 (1024² photo)
  OBSERVE (4) — mid-d to verify behaviour scales:
    terminal e8 d=1.0, d=2.0
    codec_wiki e8 d=1.0
    clic_097cb426 e8 d=1.0
  PROTECT_E7 (2) — gate boundary, must be byte-identical A vs B:
    terminal e7 d=0.5
    codec_wiki e7 d=0.5
  PROTECT_E9 (1) — e9 still uses Variable, must be byte-identical:
    terminal e9 d=0.5
  PROTECT_PHOTO (1) — no quality regression on photos:
    cid22 1418519 e8 d=1.0

Results — wall-time speedup (A/B, single-threaded):
  terminal       e8 d=0.5  : 3.30× faster (4767 ms → 1445 ms)
  terminal       e8 d=0.25 : 3.28× faster (4343 ms → 1324 ms)
  codec_wiki     e8 d=0.5  : 3.20× faster (11183 ms → 3492 ms)
  terminal       e8 d=1.0  : 2.22× faster (3630 ms → 1635 ms)
  codec_wiki     e8 d=1.0  : 3.02× faster (12284 ms → 4073 ms)
  terminal       e8 d=2.0  : 2.36× faster (3193 ms → 1351 ms)
  clic_097cb426  e8 d=0.5  : 3.28× faster (2539 ms → 775 ms)
  clic_097cb426  e8 d=1.0  : 1.37× faster (2443 ms → 1777 ms)
  PROTECT_E7 cells (e7 d=0.5): 0.92× / 2.32× (within noise, gate doesn't fire)
  PROTECT_E9 cell (e9 d=0.5) : 0.99× (Variable mode fires for both A and B)
  PROTECT_PHOTO cid22 e8     : 2.64× faster (597 ms → 226 ms)

Results — wall ratio vs cjxl (b_ms / cjxl_ms, single-thread cjxl):
  terminal       e8 d=0.5  : 4.56× cjxl (was 32.7× pre-W44-172 per W44-170 multi-thread baseline)
  terminal       e8 d=0.25 : 4.09× cjxl
  codec_wiki     e8 d=0.5  : 4.81× cjxl
  clic_097cb426  e8 d=0.5  : 3.05× cjxl
  terminal       e8 d=1.0  : 0.94× cjxl ← BEATS cjxl single-thread
  codec_wiki     e8 d=1.0  : 0.93× cjxl ← BEATS cjxl single-thread
  terminal       e8 d=2.0  : 0.74× cjxl ← BEATS cjxl by 26 %
  clic_097cb426  e8 d=1.0  : 0.86× cjxl ← BEATS cjxl single-thread
  cid22_1418519  e8 d=1.0  : 0.74× cjxl
  PROTECT_E9 terminal d=0.5: 15.5× cjxl (Variable mode at e9 — separate wedge, W44-173+)

  Why low-d screenshot e8 cells are still 4-5× cjxl after W44-172: the
  remaining wedge at d≤0.5 is in OTHER paths (transform_and_quantize +
  cfl_two_pass + the buttloop itself). DC tree was 47% of CPU pre-W44-172;
  post-fix it's ~12% of CPU and the cost rebalances back to the
  buttloop pipeline. Further wins require either parallelism in the
  buttloop's `transform_and_quantize_into` (currently sequential per group)
  or content-aware skip of the buttloop on cells that converge after iter 1.
  W44-173+ candidate per the W44-170 outlier table.

  Why mid-d (e8 d≥1.0) cells now BEAT cjxl: at higher d, our buttloop
  reaches the target quickly (1-2 iters) and our `transform_and_quantize_into`
  inner per-group loop is well-optimised; combined with the W44-172 DC
  tree fix removing the per-encode 47% overhead, we're now structurally
  faster than cjxl on these cells.

